{
  "result": "For eleven years, someone had been returning the same library book with a grocery list tucked between pages 84 and 85.\n\nThe lists were never quite the same. In winter, they called for oranges. Once, for three consecutive weeks, they called for birthday candles. The librarian, who had no right to know and every opportunity to wonder, hoped there were three birthdays and not one repeatedly forgotten.\n\nThen, one Tuesday, the list said:\n\n*Milk  \nEggs  \nSomething he\u2019ll eat*\n\nThe librarian thought about that last line all afternoon. At closing, she slipped a recipe for rice pudding into the book.\n\nThree weeks later, the book came back. The recipe was still there, with a small grease stain in one corner. On the reverse, in the same square handwriting as the lists, someone had written:\n\n*He ate it cold, standing at the fridge.*\n\nThere was no thank-you. The librarian was glad. A thank-you might have ended the conversation.\n\nShe turned the scrap over and wrote, *So did my father.*\n\nThen she put the book back on the shelf. The stranger had always known where to find it.",
